                "label": "Heat and Temperature",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "Define heat and write its SI unit. Why does heat flows from hot body to cold body?",
                    "a": "(a) Heat is that form of energy which is transferred from a hot body to a cold body due to the difference in temperature; its SI unit is joule (J). (b) Heat always flows from a body of higher temperature to a body of lower temperature, because the direction of the flow of heat is decided by the difference in temperature."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Convert 30° C into Kelvin and Fahrenheit Scale.",
                    "a": "[[S]]Convert into Kelvin:[[/S]][[E]]K = °C + 273[[/E]][[E]]K = 30 + 273 = 303 K[[/E]][[S]]Convert into Fahrenheit:[[/S]][[E]]°F = 1.8°C + 32[[/E]][[E]]°F = 1.8 × 30 + 32 = 54 + 32 = 86°F[[/E]][[A]]30°C = 303 K = 86°F.[[/A]]"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Explain three different scales of temperature along with their main uses. Differentiate between heat and temperature.",
                    "a": "(a) (1) Celsius scale — mostly used for environmental measurements. (2) Fahrenheit scale — mostly used for clinical measurements. (3) Kelvin scale — mostly used for industrial measurements. (b) Heat is a form of energy which is transferred from one body to another; its SI unit is joule. Temperature is the degree of hotness or coldness of a body; it decides the direction of the flow of heat; its SI unit is kelvin."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Convert 212°F into Celsius and Kelvin.",
                    "a": "[[S]]Convert into Celsius:[[/S]][[E]]°C = (°F − 32) / 1.8[[/E]][[E]]°C = (212 − 32) / 1.8 = 180 / 1.8 = 100°C[[/E]][[S]]Convert into Kelvin:[[/S]][[E]]K = °C + 273[[/E]][[E]]K = 100 + 273 = 373 K[[/E]][[A]]212°F = 100°C = 373 K.[[/A]]"
                  }
                ]
